    },
    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Schwimmen',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Schwimmen (31) is a Commerce-group card game in which players hold three cards and improve their hand by exchanging with a central pool of three face-up cards. The aim is to collect cards of one suit totalling as high as possible (maximum 31) or three of a kind. Played with chips; the loser of each hand loses a chip and the last survivor wins. Popular in Germany and Austria.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Country/Region of Origin: Austria and Germany (particularly western Austria and Germany). Known in many parts of the world.\nTime Period: Early 1900s\nHistorical Development: The game evolved from traditional card games in the region; German name Schwimmen (swimming) refers to the last chance before a player drops out.\nCultural Significance: Popular social and family game in Austria and Germany',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Minimum Players: 2\nMaximum Players: 6\nOptimal Players: 4\nPartnerships: Optional',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Type: 32-card deck (strip 2-6 from standard pack). Cards 7-10, J, Q, K, A in four suits. One deck.\nNumber of Decks: 1\nJokers: Not included',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '32-card deck (or strip 2-6 from standard deck)\nChips or tokens (e.g. 3 per player)\nTable space',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Dealer gives 3 cards face down to each player plus one extra hand of 3 cards. Dealer chooses to keep their own 3 or the extra hand (before seeing the extra hand). The rejected 3 cards are turned face up and placed in the centre as the table pool. Dealing pattern: clockwise.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Dutch 31: You must exchange or close each turn (no pass). Closing does not end the hand; play continues until only two remain, then one may close. Declaring 31 ends the hand immediately. No Feuer; three aces score 30.5. Often scored with dice (start at 6); first to 0 gets the Chicken (one extra life).',
                  },
                  {
                    text: '41: Four cards each and four face-up on table (52-card pack); max hand value 41. Options: swap one card, swap all four, replace table and then swap one, or end the game. Winner gains points equal to hand value; losers lose the difference. Ties with winner also win.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
